>One issue: This utility has its own version of some of the I2C
>kernel driver modules, and it is reported that this might cause
>problems if you are also trying to use the regular modules for
>other purposes.  I forget who said it (off in a mail folder,
>somewhere), but somebody on the list said he'd look at the code
>to try to solve this issue.

Even if there are kernel symbol conflicts. I think we can all agree
that preventing a $15,000 machine from catching fire is more 
important than any other I2C monitoring that may be going on :-)

>Another issue: I compiled and looked through the package, and it
>appears it can shut down the operating system, but I did not see
>anything that obviously would physically turn off the power
>supply.

On UP2000 it wouldn't be able to even if the code was there. It's
I2C bus was not as advanced as the 2000+ and CS20. The last two
OTOH 'can' be switched off via I2C though like Rich said, it was not
set as default behavior. 

Moreover, Considering Rich's next post. I think you're right about
the CH320 chassis though now that I think about it. The wiring
could be changed to accommodate ATX style power cycling. Though
it would probably void your warranty :-(. 

>Still, it's a "cool" utility.  :-)

Yeah it is. Just don't get 'poll' happy with it and have it check the
temperature every 5 secs. Under extreme load the utility would time
out and 'somehow' read back an insane temperature of either zero,
below zero or something extremely high. In other words, defeating
the purpose of the utility. 

Peter
