> From: Helge Kreutzmann <kreutzm@itp.uni-hannover.de>
> Date: Thu, 16 May 2002 09:42:31 +0200
>
> We have several XP1000 and xlock kills console access on it, e.g. if
> xlock has run for a while then the console no longer responds. The
> monitor does not display anything, nor seem keystrokes to have an
> effect. The only way to restore display is to shut down the machine,
> turn it off (otherwise display remains blank) and turn it back on. The
> only symptom I could see is that X is eating 100% cpu time when this
> happens. The machine is perfectly accessible over the net however.
>
> This happens both with XFree 4.1 and 4.2. The machines are build alike
> with
>
> Display controller: Texas Instruments TVP4020 [Permedia 2] (rev 01)
>
> I am running out of ideas where to look for the problem. Any hints
> (aside the obvious and non ideal of not locking the screen) are
> very welcome.

RH7.1's xlockmore package (xlockmore-4.17.2-2, I think) was _not_
compiled with the -mieee option.  This makes it vulnerable to
floating point exceptions (signal 8, IIRC).  You might try
compiling it with this option.  There is a Redhat bugzilla report
under my email address with more detail.

Especially if you compiled your own X server, the -mieee option
is also needed for the X server.  I compiled XFree86 4.2.0 from
xfree86.org sources, without this option, and I'm seeing the X
server blown away on occasion when running xlock.  In my case,
the X server (or xlock before recompiling) would simply exit and
leave the display unlocked or thrown back to the login prompt.
With a different situation, I would think it might be possible
for the floating point exceptions to cause your symptoms--but
I'm very far from an expert, so, ...

Good luck.

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com




