--- "William H. Magill" <magill@isc.upenn.edu> wrote:
> >   How about Compaq? they have been selling 1U
> Alpha DS-10L for while.
> >   well, Compaq -> HP made Apple forget about ???
> >
> >   Apple's promotional items ALWAYS tends to have a
> lot of misleading...
> >
> EVERYBODY's marketing hype is always based on
> half-truths...
> Remember the definition of "industry" is the key
> here -- as well as "what
> the meaning of 'is,' is."
> 
> But saddly, the issue is even worse than that -- The
> ONLY reason that Apple 
> could even contemplate such a statement (and they
> probably made it in good
> faith, which is the really scary part)

Yet, even as I and another axp-list participant have
contacted Apple regarding the misleading statement two
days ago (even offering API CS20 URLs), it still
remains on the Apple Xserve website:

     http://www.apple.com/xserve/

That more than one person has provided Apple with the
facts and that they continue to offer the erroneous
statement on their site is NOT an example of good
faith.

That's simply false advertising.

, is because
> NOBODY, except existing
> Alpha customers, have a clue that the DS-10L exists.
> Alpha is hardly a
> household word, even in the Unix community. Compaq
> never managed to
> generate any kind of "mind-share" for Alpha and
> consequently they never
> managed to sell any. 

Well, both API and Compaq share some blame regarding
the poor marketing of the DS-10L and CS20 (and Alpha
in general) though Compaq does bear the greater
proportion.

I believe it's apparent that Compaq only wanted the
customers and was eager to ditch the technology.  Back
in 1999, our former Compaq rep even suggested that we
consider replacing some of the DU4.0 boxes with
Windows NT/i386!

> 
> It's very sad, but very true.
> 
> And by the way, I haven't heard anybody mention the
> Netra. It's Sun's 1u
> SPARC box that's been out for over a year.

It's a nifty box but is uniprocessor - to its credit
Apple claims to have introduced the "industry's first
1U dual processor server."

> 
> And in response to someone's comment about
> "preaching to the choir."
> As I understand things - that statement is very
> true. Interestingly, Apple
> didn't/doesn't anticipate any "conflict/competition"
> between other vendors
> products and the Xserv.  They seem to be genuinely
> surprised at the
> attention its getting.

I'm not so sure about that... I'd say that a greater
proportion of computer users equate "Anti-Microsoft"
and "Anti-Intel" with "Apple."

Especially in the education market, there is still
very strong loyalty to Apple despite the popularity of
free operating systems like Linux/Net/FreeBSD and a
plethora of non-i386 equipment.  Apple realises this
and obviously wants to capitalise on this.

I work in a university as well.  There's a lot of
excitement among my coworkers about ditching the DEC,
SGI and Sun boxes for these Xserves, sight unseen. 
Some are already preparing requisitions and porting
software on desktop G4 PowerMacs as of yesterday
afternoon.  It's even more strange as they complain
about the unproven nature of Windows 2k/XP and yet
rave on about the unproven Mac OS X Server to replace
the proven Tru64/IRIX/Solaris/Linux infrastructure
we've built.

It's going to be a strange future - a shade of blue
that is not at all pleasing.  If you don't believe me,
I leave you with just one final acronym: HFS+

sousa


