>   From: sousa jack <sousa_jack@yahoo.com>
>
>   I work in a university as well.  There's a lot of
>   excitement among my coworkers about ditching the DEC,
>   SGI and Sun boxes for these Xserves, sight unseen. 
>   Some are already preparing requisitions and porting
>   software on desktop G4 PowerMacs as of yesterday
>   afternoon.  It's even more strange as they complain
>   about the unproven nature of Windows 2k/XP and yet
>   rave on about the unproven Mac OS X Server to replace
>   the proven Tru64/IRIX/Solaris/Linux infrastructure
>   we've built.
>
The reason that DEC had, and Sun still has, a major presence in the
Education market is exactly the same reason that Apple once did, and is
working to regain. All 3 vendors were believers in the marketing adage -- 
"Get'em while they're young." That is - if students grow up using Sun,
they'll buy Sun when they get into the real world. At the end, DEC forget
their roots and Compaq never even acknowledged them. (developing CSLG was
like pulling teeth, and the "hobbiest" licenses for VMS and Tru64 came from
DECUS bitching, marketing fought against them.)

OSX server really isn't "unproven" by any stretch of the imagination.
Especaially in a headless, and therfore GUI-less environment. It runs the
same Mach kernel that Tru64 is built on. If you ever played with YP or NIS,
(let alone NeXT) then Netinfo is no harder to comprehend than C2 on Tru64.
And quite frankly, I'm much more at home with OS X than with Linux.
The only difference between OS X Server and Client is that the Server has
GUIs to run Apache, Quicktime and Sendmail. The only "unique" server is the
file server, unless you normally run SAMBA or the old CAP package, and then
it is familiar as well.

The biggest problem that OS X has is the same one that Linux has -- the
file system can't hold a candle to AdvFS. Someday, both will have journaled
file systems as standard and even that problem will go away. But even that
is a moot point, because by the time that happens, FileSystems will be a
historical artifact... being replaced with true virtual storage served up
from a SAN. (Compaq calls theirs Versastore and Linux won't leave UFS
behind before it's a serious product.)

And besides... Apple snubbed API a long time ago. OS X has been running on
Alpha for about 3 years now -- since Rhapsody days (and Darwin compiles on
Alpha today). If Apple had chosen Alpha as the chip for the future, we 
probably would all be much happier today. ... As I recall, Apple almost
sells more Macs in a month than all of the Alphas Compaq and Digital
combined ever sold. 

