storri@ameritech.net said:
> I am familiar with building the kernel on an intel platform. The thing
> that I am stuck on is the equivalent make commands to: 

"make boot" creates the bootable image arch/alpha/vmlinux.gz. Just
copy this to /boot/vmlinux-2.4.18.gz or whereever you want to put
this image (don't replace your current kernel!) and edit your
/etc/aboot.conf file to include an entry for your new kernel. Mine
looks like this:

# aboot default configurations
# NOTICE:  You have a /boot partition.  This means that
#          all kernel paths are relative to /boot/
0:1/vmlinuz-2.4.3-12 root=/dev/sda2
1:1/vmlinuz-2.4.3-12 root=/dev/sda2 console=ttyS0
2:1/vmlinuz-2.4.3-12 root=/dev/sda2 console=ttyS0 console=tty0
3:1/vmlinuz-2.4.3-12 root=/dev/sda2 single
4:1/vmlinuz-2.4.3-12 root=/dev/sda2 floppy=nodma,nofifo
5:1/vmlinuz-2.4.18 root=/dev/sda2
6:1/vmlinuz-2.4.18 root=/dev/sda2 single
7:1/vmlinuz-2.4.9-31 root=/dev/sda2 single

Notice that I have 8 possible boot options in this file. Many of
these were installed yb the initial install. #5 is the one I added
for my 2.4.18 kernel. Also nite that /boot on my machine is a
partition of its own so the file names are relative the root
of that partition.

Anyhow, to boot this kernel from srm, I do this at the srm console:

  boot dk0 -fl 5

to go right into the kernel, or

  boot dk0 -fl i

to go into interactive mode for aboot. (I forget the exact device name,
I have to look it up every time, because I reboot so rarely:-)

The PC164 doesn't have the mythical halt button (never seen one) so
automatic reboot is not an option for PC164/srm. You must therefore
have seen at least some of the "boot" command by now. The trick here
is where to find (and where to install) the bootable kernel image,
and how to tell aboot about it.
