On Fri, May 17, 2002 at 08:34:42PM -0700, Stephen Williams wrote:
> 
> 
> Anyhow, to boot this kernel from srm, I do this at the srm console:
> 
>   boot dk0 -fl 5
> 
> to go right into the kernel, or
> 
>   boot dk0 -fl i
> 
> to go into interactive mode for aboot. (I forget the exact device name,
> I have to look it up every time, because I reboot so rarely:-)

While at SRM prompt put your default boot device name into
'bootdef_dev' variable, using 'set', and then you do not have remember
and you can shorten commands above to:

  b -fl 5

and

  b -fl i

You can still override that with an explicit device name if you
want to boot from something else.

Check with 'show boo*' at SRM prompt to see which boot related variables
are set and how and 'show auto*' to see how 'auto_action' looks like.

Even if you want always boot interactively you can set later to BOOT
and 'boot_osflags' (? - check name) to 'i'.  This will land you on
a power up at 'aboot>' prompt where you can pick which of predefined
boot commands to use or you can type explicitely still something
different or go back to SRM with 'q'.

  Michal




