That is actually not too bright advice ...

> Well, from that point on, it became completely imposible to boot the machine
> from that partition.

... as you just found.  The idea is that in this way you can create
a FAT file system on your hard disk without doing any formatting or
anything of that sort.  The downside is that you will get a floppy size
file system, even if this partition is seriously bigger,  a type can
be slightly wrong and if there are some imperfections on a floppy, even
if areas which are not really used, you may get screwed.

> The strange thing is that it boots without problem
> from the same milo and ldmilo.exe files (at least its checksums are equal)
> provided that they are in a floppy.

Just boot from this floppy, blow away junk in /dev/sda1, make FAT
file system on it ('man mkdosfs') of a partition size, mount floppy
and this partition and _copy_ these two files from a floppy to a hard
drive; or you can use 'mcopy' without mounting anything.

  Michal




