Those who like to live on the edge may be interested in rpm packages
of kernels for Alpha based on 2.4.18-4 (sources, binaries and also
separately patches).  This the current kernel used in Red Hat 7.3
distribution for x86 boxes.  Alpha version can be found at

  ftp://ftp.harddata.com/pub/kernel_Nautilus/2.4.18-4/

"Nautilus" above does not mean "Nautilus only" but only that support
for Nautilus boards was specifically re-hacked.  A type specific
code for other variants was not touched.  Sorry, no Jensen ready
binaries.  If somebody still runs that and really wants this kernel
then a recompilation of included sources is an option. OTOH binaries
for SMP machines are there.  Other interesting piece for those
with QLogic controllers could be an included driver from
www.feral.com.  I do not have such hardware so I can only hope
that this works but chances are pretty high.

This is not the same as a "mainstream" 2.4.18 or even 2.4.19-pre...
In particular a scheduler is different which required some other
changes.  It is also not something to bug Red Hat with at they never
released _that_.  WORKSFORME(TM) and some others but you will be
still a guinea-pig and on your own.  You have been warned.

Please start from a lecture of READMEs.  In particular they list
various user-space requirements and is likely that you will have to
update some bits and pieces.   Make friends with "rawhide" and/or
your compiler.

Binaries are _not_ configured to work with milos.  If you are
booting that way then either you need a working milo version able to
start kernels with a higher load address or you have to recompile
sources after setting CONFIG_ALPHA_LEGACY_START_ADDRESS=y.  This is
not hard.  Install 'src.rpm', edit in SOURCES kernel config file you
are going to use and, possibly, spec file to compile only those
kernels you really want and 'rpmbuild -ba kernel-2.4.hd.spec'.  It
should finish after a (somewhat longer :-) while and there is
a chance that it will even boot.

  Happy hacking,
  Michal

p.s.  ftp.harddata.com will not accept connections from hosts which
"do not exist" as far as DNS is concerned or have messed up DNS
entries where forward and reverse resolutions do not agree.




